6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Are you ready to count down to our number one mega-marvel machine?

7
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:35,240
We're cutting, we're crushing, and cruising like never before.

8
00:00:35,241 --> 00:00:38,740
It's awe-inspiring machines, now on this edition

9
00:00:38,741 --> 00:00:41,240
of Modern Marvel's Mega Marvels Countdown.

10
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:57,760
Our fast-paced 21st century lives rely on the mechanized world.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,500
Over the years, Modern Marvels has given you

12
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,000
a unique behind-the-scenes look at these amazing machines

13
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:08,540
that play a part in our day-to-day existence.

14
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:11,140
From the massive factory assembly lines

15
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:14,040
to the technological marvels of the military,

16
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:17,060
and everything in between.

17
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,460
From our vast archives, here are our top 10 most awe-inspiring

18
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:23,560
machines of all times.

19
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:27,700
And we're counting down to number one.

20
00:01:27,701 --> 00:01:30,340
Launching us off is one of the most unique watercrafts

21
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,040
we've ever seen.

22
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:37,280
At number 10 is the Sea Breacher X.

23
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:39,540
If you've ever wanted to swim with sharks,

24
00:01:39,640 --> 00:01:41,940
here's your chance.

25
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:43,620
This boat is about the closest you're

26
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:48,020
going to get to a sensation of a dolphin or a shark.

27
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:50,620
You go through the water, you jump over it,

28
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:55,700
you can totally do anything that you can imagine.

29
00:01:55,701 --> 00:01:58,540
Robert Innes and his team at Innes Face Productions

30
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,700
in Redding, California, have designed

31
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,640
the ultimate aquatic thrill ride, the Sea Breacher X.

32
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:11,440
This high-octane machine was born out of Innes' goal

33
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:12,860
to build an underwater jet ski.

34
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:18,660
This one is our most extreme vehicle.

35
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:21,500
It puts out about 255 horsepower.

36
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:24,260
It's capable of about 50 miles an hour on the surface.

37
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:29,400
At the shop, the next fleet of custom-made shark and dolphin

38
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,540
boats are on the line.

39
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:34,300
The shells and motors arrive prefabricated,

40
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:35,440
and the work begins.

41
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:40,440
Welders machine every part based on the specs

42
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,640
of the ordered boat.

43
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:46,660
With a base model going for $80,000,

44
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:50,500
our number 10 machine is not your average family speedboat.

45
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:53,060
You know, we have a pretty diverse clientele of people

46
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:54,160
who buy these.

47
00:02:54,161 --> 00:02:58,960
You know, we have everything from wealthy Arab clients

48
00:02:58,961 --> 00:03:01,960
to adrenaline junkies.

49
00:03:01,961 --> 00:03:03,660
The buoyancy of the sea breacher

50
00:03:03,661 --> 00:03:08,000
is carefully designed to remain consistent so it won't sink.

51
00:03:08,001 --> 00:03:10,000
So within the walls of the sea breacher,

52
00:03:10,001 --> 00:03:12,620
there's actually flotation, which is urethane foam.

53
00:03:12,621 --> 00:03:15,120
So even if the whole cockpit fills with water,

54
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:17,120
the foam will keep you afloat.

55
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:19,320
So it's a safety feature built into the fuselage

56
00:03:19,321 --> 00:03:20,820
so it can't sink.

57
00:03:20,821 --> 00:03:22,320
Famous last words.

58
00:03:22,321 --> 00:03:24,120
While it's totally submersible,

59
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:26,520
the Sea Breacher is also equipped with impressive

60
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:28,460
aerodynamic capabilities.

61
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:32,060
These are much like the marine mammals that inspired the craft.

62
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:34,060
And this mashup of man and machine

63
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:37,060
is another reason the Sea Breacher comes in at number 10

64
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:39,880
on our awe-inspiring machines countdown.

65
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:41,680
These are not submarines.

66
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:42,980
They fly underwater.

67
00:03:43,080 --> 00:03:46,080
So it's really like a cross between a boat and a plane.

68
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:51,520
Now, a regular airplane has wings that

69
00:03:51,521 --> 00:03:53,360
that suck it up into the sky,

70
00:03:53,361 --> 00:03:56,360
so we use forward momentum and downwards-facing wings

71
00:03:56,361 --> 00:03:58,520
to suck it underneath the water.

72
00:03:58,521 --> 00:04:00,520
If you push these boats forward,

73
00:04:00,521 --> 00:04:02,860
it's forcing the entire boat underwater.

74
00:04:02,861 --> 00:04:04,360
It's giving negative lift.

75
00:04:04,361 --> 00:04:05,860
If you pull these boats back,

76
00:04:05,861 --> 00:04:08,280
it's actually bringing it up above the surface.

77
00:04:10,100 --> 00:04:12,280
You actually do a lot of the controlling of the sea breacher

78
00:04:12,281 --> 00:04:13,480
with your feet here.

79
00:04:13,481 --> 00:04:15,180
You can hitch the nose up and down

80
00:04:15,181 --> 00:04:16,980
by pointing your toes forward.

81
00:04:16,981 --> 00:04:18,520
That points it into a dive,

82
00:04:18,521 --> 00:04:20,140
and when you push your heels like that,

83
00:04:20,141 --> 00:04:21,340
that brings the nose up.

84
00:04:21,341 --> 00:04:22,640
That's how you get the big jumps.

85
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:29,140
But acrobatics like this couldn't

86
00:04:29,141 --> 00:04:32,680
happen without a boatload of horsepower.

87
00:04:32,681 --> 00:04:34,260
This particular motor is a three-cylinder.

88
00:04:34,261 --> 00:04:38,260
It's a four-stroke engine, and it runs on regular gasoline.

89
00:04:38,261 --> 00:04:39,260
Fire it up.

90
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:43,260
You need a lot of horsepower to get these things

91
00:04:43,261 --> 00:04:45,760
under the water.

92
00:04:45,761 --> 00:04:48,660
Typical family ski boat is about 200 horse.

93
00:04:48,661 --> 00:04:53,160
So here we have like a 16-foot canoe with 250 horsepower.

94
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:56,400
So obviously, it's going to haul it.

95
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:59,540
This ingenious fusion of throttle thrust and horsepower

96
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:03,140
allows for performance never before seen in a watercraft

97
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:08,180
and propels the Sea Breacher X at number 10 on our countdown.

98
00:05:08,280 --> 00:05:10,740
Coolest thing for me is just flying underwater.

99
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,520
People say, you know, you're down there sightseeing,

100
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:13,660
can you see fish?

101
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:15,760
You just get tunnel vision because everything

102
00:05:15,860 --> 00:05:17,560
is happening so quickly.

103
00:05:17,561 --> 00:05:19,800
It's kind of like strapping yourself to a torpedo.

104
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:30,600
From the aquatic thrills of the sea breacher at number 10,

105
00:05:30,601 --> 00:05:32,800
we're amping up the raw horsepower

106
00:05:32,801 --> 00:05:35,920
for our next awe-inspiring machine.

107
00:05:35,921 --> 00:05:40,520
At number 9, these ferocious beasts rule the track,

108
00:05:40,521 --> 00:05:42,520
the world's strongest trackers.

109
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:47,320
These fire-breathing monsters have gathered

110
00:05:47,321 --> 00:05:49,320
at the National Tractor Pulling Championship

111
00:05:49,321 --> 00:05:51,320
in Bowling Green, Ohio.

112
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:56,320
It's got a mind of its own.

113
00:05:56,321 --> 00:05:59,320
Once it throttles down, you're along for the ride.

114
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:03,320
Here, these souped-up tractors vie for the title

115
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:06,320
of World's Strongest.

116
00:06:06,321 --> 00:06:08,320
It's a big rush. It's fun.

117
00:06:08,321 --> 00:06:10,320
We may only hit 30, 40 miles an hour,

118
00:06:10,321 --> 00:06:12,320
but it feels like they're going 250.

119
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:16,320
The goal is simple.

120
00:06:16,321 --> 00:06:20,020
Pull an ever-increasing load of up to 60,000 pounds

121
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:22,860
and go until you can't move the weight any further.

122
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:29,960
Tractor pulls are divided into 15 classes,

123
00:06:30,060 --> 00:06:32,900
but it's the unlimited modified class that brings out the

124
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:34,680
strongest and wildest.

125
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:41,480
Limited only by their imagination and pocketbook,

126
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:44,140
these pullers build their masterpieces from the ground

127
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:46,320
up.

128
00:06:46,321 --> 00:06:49,660
These here tractors, they're all, you make them yourself

129
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:52,560
in your own shop, and there is not two unlimited tractors

130
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:53,560
the same.

131
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:55,560
Everybody's got a different combination.

132
00:06:55,660 --> 00:06:57,860
The winning formula comes down to the same reason

133
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:01,260
these mega tractors are number nine on our countdown,

134
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:03,680
raw engine power.

135
00:07:03,780 --> 00:07:05,580
There are three main types of engines

136
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,480
used in the unlimited class.

137
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,020
There's the classic V8, just a little more hopped up.

138
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:14,880
Each tractor can have up to four V8 engines.

139
00:07:14,881 --> 00:07:19,600
Then there's the V12 Allison engine, pulled from World War II fighter planes.

140
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:22,560
It's a little harder to find, but worth the wait.

141
00:07:24,060 --> 00:07:30,600
And for the ultimate in thrust, there's the jet turbines that might have powered a Chinook helicopter not too long ago.

142
00:07:31,600 --> 00:07:39,600
What we have in our unlimited modified tractor is four what we call automotive-type V8s.

143
00:07:39,601 --> 00:07:44,600
You won't find these engines in any automobile made today or ever made.

144
00:07:44,601 --> 00:07:47,600
These are pretty much a thoroughbred racing engine.

145
00:07:47,601 --> 00:07:54,600
Each engine will dyno out at approximately 2,800 to 3,000 horsepower.

146
00:07:54,601 --> 00:07:57,600
They run on straight methanol for fuel.

147
00:07:57,601 --> 00:08:00,600
They're supercharged, fuel injected.

148
00:08:00,601 --> 00:08:08,600
As they line up for the full pull, these monsters of the track pack more than 12,000 horsepower.

149
00:08:08,601 --> 00:08:11,300
But they're pulling a sled with an ever-increasing load up

150
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:15,000
to 60,000 pounds, designed to break the momentum of even

151
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:17,600
the most powerful forward thrust.

152
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:21,140
In the Unlimited class, we weigh 7,900 pounds.

153
00:08:21,240 --> 00:08:24,740
And we're pulling a sled that probably weighs 60,000 pounds.

154
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:26,560
So it's quite a tug of war.

155
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:28,260
There's no clock to contend with.

156
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:30,060
It's all about distance.

157
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,360
The winner is the last vehicle moving.

158
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:38,160
The Sport may use engines that generate thousands

159
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:42,900
of horsepower, but its origins go straight back to the horse.

160
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,800
Tractor pulling's origins came from the 1920s, late 20s,

161
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:49,840
when farmers were actually starting to get horses,

162
00:08:49,940 --> 00:08:51,200
and they would have these horse pulls.

163
00:08:51,300 --> 00:08:52,340
Who had the strongest horse?

164
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:53,880
Then along came tractors.

165
00:08:53,980 --> 00:08:56,820
Who had the strongest tractors?

166
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:59,580
Today, the long-held horsepower contest of noise

167
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:01,420
and adrenaline lives on.

168
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:04,280
In a uniquely American event, the showcase

169
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:06,460
is the remarkable machine you get when

170
00:09:06,461 --> 00:09:10,960
when you mix human ingenuity with unlimited engine power.

171
00:09:10,961 --> 00:09:12,460
It's just an adrenaline rush.

172
00:09:12,461 --> 00:09:15,460
It's something that is really an American icon.

173
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,740
Engine power got us going with the amazing Sea Breacher,

174
00:09:23,741 --> 00:09:26,320
coming in at number 10 on our countdown.

175
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:30,120
And we powered into number nine with supercharged tractors.

176
00:09:30,121 --> 00:09:33,760
Coming up, we're going even hotter for number eight,

177
00:09:33,761 --> 00:09:36,900
with a machine that's sure to leave you smoldering with awe.

178
00:09:36,901 --> 00:09:39,360
You can burn the roof off the place.

179
00:09:39,361 --> 00:09:42,300
We're counting down to our number one machine,

180
00:09:42,301 --> 00:09:45,480
a visionary contraption that comes from the mind of a man

181
00:09:45,481 --> 00:09:47,840
some call a true mad scientist.

182
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:55,340
We're celebrating the very best from the world of machines

183
00:09:55,341 --> 00:09:58,560
by counting down the top 10 of those mechanical marvels

184
00:09:58,561 --> 00:10:01,460
of speed, power, and ingenuity.

185
00:10:01,461 --> 00:10:04,300
The aquatic thrills of the totally unique Sea Breacher

186
00:10:04,301 --> 00:10:06,960
started us off at number 10, followed

187
00:10:06,961 --> 00:10:10,120
by the extreme horsepower of the world's strongest tractors

188
00:10:10,121 --> 00:10:11,640
at number nine.

189
00:10:11,641 --> 00:10:13,880
We've got eight more awe-inspiring machines

190
00:10:13,881 --> 00:10:17,240
to go before we get to number one.

191
00:10:17,241 --> 00:10:20,440
And we're stepping it up with a wild and crazy.

192
00:10:20,441 --> 00:10:25,280
At number eight, it's the truly one of a kind Flame Tornado.

193
00:10:25,281 --> 00:10:26,780
This is the flame tornado.

194
00:10:26,781 --> 00:10:29,180
It can be small, or it can burn the roof off the place

195
00:10:29,181 --> 00:10:30,220
if I turn it all the way up.

196
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:34,060
Kevin Binkert owns Standard Metal Products,

197
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:36,340
a machining business in San Francisco,

198
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:38,700
where they walk a fine line between making

199
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:41,200
the practical and the bizarre.

200
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:44,140
But Binkert's most outrageous creation yet

201
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,200
was the spawn of his subconscious.

202
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:49,220
It's a piece that I made in the early 90s

203
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:54,320
after having a powerful dream of a fire vortex.

204
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:58,120
By using aluminum blades, a 20-gallon propane tank,

205
00:10:58,121 --> 00:11:02,960
a small 1.5-horsepower engine, and a single match,

206
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:06,520
Binkert is able to create this jaw-dropping, towering inferno

207
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:08,660
that's well-earned its spot at number eight

208
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:09,860
on our machine's countdown.

209
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:15,140
Well, it works a lot like a regular tornado

210
00:11:15,240 --> 00:11:17,840
where it's pulling air in from the bottom.

211
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:20,200
So the simple answer to what's happening here

212
00:11:20,300 --> 00:11:21,840
is that we've got a series of blades

213
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:24,680
that are creating a rotating column of air.

214
00:11:24,780 --> 00:11:28,120
You want laminar flow, so there's not a lot of chaotic air.

215
00:11:28,121 --> 00:11:31,360
And as the air rushes in, convection's pulling it up,

216
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:36,600
and it's a self-perpetuating vortex of flame.

217
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:37,860
The result?

218
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:42,860
A blazing cyclone of fire reaching up to 40 feet in height.

219
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:46,240
While the flame tornado is a big hit at Kevin's workshop,

220
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:49,780
the locals have yet to embrace its awesome potential.

221
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:54,920
And ideally, the neighbors don't call the fire department.

222
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:56,740
San Francisco Fire Department

223
00:11:56,741 --> 00:12:00,040
department was in fact called during this demonstration,

224
00:12:00,140 --> 00:12:03,040
not a first for Kevin Binker.

225
00:12:03,140 --> 00:12:07,580
The matter was quickly resolved this time around.

226
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:10,680
But don't try the fire tornado at home.

227
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:16,600
This awe-inspiring machine is just too hot to handle.

228
00:12:16,700 --> 00:12:19,740
From burning down the house with the fire vortex at number eight

229
00:12:19,840 --> 00:12:22,440
on our countdown of awe-inspiring machines,

230
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:25,840
here's a machine with even more destructive power.

231
00:12:25,841 --> 00:12:31,640
Coming in at number seven is the Extreme Metal Shredder.

232
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:35,180
In Elkhart, Indiana, lives a mechanical beast that

233
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:38,080
can chew up a four-door sedan in seconds.

234
00:12:42,180 --> 00:12:46,200
Well, what we've got is an 8,000 horsepower oversized paper

235
00:12:46,300 --> 00:12:47,100
shredder.

236
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:51,600
And it's fed by a conveyor that's effectively a 200-foot long,

237
00:12:51,700 --> 00:12:53,900
eight-foot wide bulldozer track.

238
00:12:53,901 --> 00:12:56,640
takes the car bodies up.

239
00:12:56,740 --> 00:12:58,600
They go down into the shredder, and like your mother's

240
00:12:58,700 --> 00:13:01,380
old ringer washer, there's a set of rollers that push the cars

241
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:02,780
into the shredder.

242
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:05,180
It is all shredded down to small pieces,

243
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:07,480
through grates in the bottom like an oversized cheese

244
00:13:07,580 --> 00:13:09,120
grater.

245
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:11,720
At Sturgis Iron and Metal, truckloads

246
00:13:11,820 --> 00:13:15,420
of junked cars and scrap metal file in day in, day out

247
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:18,920
to feed this insatiable beast.

248
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:23,900
It can consume up to 480 cars an hour, four times the hour

249
00:13:23,901 --> 00:13:26,660
of conventional shredders, reducing vehicles

250
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:30,540
to fist-sized pieces of twisted scrap.

251
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:33,140
Inside the machine is a giant rotor.

252
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:36,440
It's mounted with two dozen 1,200-pound hammers,

253
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:40,140
rotating at 450 revolutions per minute.

254
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:44,460
The hammers slam into the scrap at 175 miles per hour,

255
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:48,160
pulverizing entire cars into tiny pieces.

